; A timeclock posting is registered with its account once, as every
; other posting is, so %(count), any() and all() over an account's
; postings see it once.  With a bucket account the bucket posting is
; registered once as well, and a balance assertion on the bucket
; still sees the timeclock amount, whether assertions run in date
; order (the default) or in file order.

bucket Equity:Time

i 2013/03/28 10:00:00 Projects:Consulting
o 2013/03/28 14:00:00

2013/03/29 Check
    Equity:Time            0s = -14400s
    Projects:Consulting    0s

i 2013/03/30 22:00:00 Projects:Admin
o 2013/03/31 02:00:00

test bal --format "%(account) %(to_int(count))\n"
Equity:Time 3
Projects 3
Projects:Admin 1
Projects:Consulting 2
 6
end test

test bal --check-in-file-order --format "%(account) %(to_int(count))\n"
Equity:Time 3
Projects 3
Projects:Admin 1
Projects:Consulting 2
 6
end test

test bal --day-break --format "%(account) %(to_int(count))\n"
Equity:Time 4
Projects 4
Projects:Admin 2
Projects:Consulting 2
 8
end test

test reg
13-Mar-28                       Projects:Consulting           4.00h        4.00h
                                Equity:Time                  -4.00h            0
13-Mar-30                       Projects:Admin                4.00h        4.00h
                                Equity:Time                  -4.00h            0
end test

test bal
              -8.00h  Equity:Time
               8.00h  Projects
               4.00h    Admin
               4.00h    Consulting
--------------------
                   0
end test
